        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Caries is the decay of the dental hard tissues (enamel, cementum, and dentin) due to the effects of oral bacteria on fermentable carbohydrates on the tooth surface.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The word “caries” is Latin for rottenness and is both the singular and plural form.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Oral bacteria ferment carbohydrates on the tooth surface, resulting in the production of acids leading to demineralization of the hard tissues, thus allowing bacterial and leukocytic digestion of the organic matrix of the tooth.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Caries has been very common in humans in “westernized” society, where diets rich in highly refined carbohydrates are the norm. Aggressive public education and preventive measures have resulted in a decline in incidence over the past several decades.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In humans,Streptococcus mutansis particularly implicated in the development of caries.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "For various reasons (e.g., diet lower in refined carbohydrates, higher salivary pH, lower salivary amylase, conical crown shape, wider interdental spacing, different indigenous oral flora), caries is not common in the domestic dog, but it does occur and should be looked for.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A study published in theJournal of",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Reading) reported that 5.3% of dogs 1 year of",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "age or older had one or more caries lesions,",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "with 52% having bilaterally symmetric",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "lesions.",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Caries can affect the crown or roots of the",
              "pdf_page": 446,
              "printed_page": 364,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Veterinary Dentistryin 1998 (see Suggested"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
